  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Developing mitigation strategies to Tiger-Rhino and other wildlife-vehicle collisions in Kaziranga Tiger Reserve, Assam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kaziranga National Park (KNP), is a critical protected area in India for rhinos, tigers, Asian elephants and other endangered wildlife. Nearly 60 kilometers of the National Highway 37 (NH 37) passes adjacent to KNP, and it is at least 7.5 meters wide. Extensive flooding during the rainy season increases wildlife crossings of NH 37. The adverse consequences to wildlife from 2- to 4-lane highway expansion include, increased vehicular traffic volumes increasing wildlife-vehicle collisions, a barrier to animal crossing, and, the road widening rendering the existing at-grade crossings of the key elephant corridors potentially ineffective. Project objectives are to, (1) Identify and prioritize highway stretches that require mitigation measures to reduce wildlife-vehicle collisions and provide safe crossing for wildlife based on: (i) crash and carcass data, (ii) observation data of wildlife seen alive on and along the highway segments, and, (iii) maps and local expert knowledge of important wildlife habitat and corridors bisected by the NH 37; (2) Recommend mitigation measures for wildlife at the selected locations; and, (3) Conduct cost-estimate analyses for a range of mitigation measures for the selected highway segments.</narrative>
  </description>
